**Problem.** Find all $f:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$ with
$$f\bigl(f(xy)+2xy\bigr)=3x\,f(y)+3y\,f(x),\qquad \forall x,y>0.\tag{P}$$

---

### Step 1. A key identity

Let $c=f(1)$. Substituting $x=1$ into (P):
$$f\bigl(f(y)+2y\bigr)=3f(y)+3cy.\tag{1}$$

Now compare (P) with (1) applied to $u=xy$. Both left–hand sides are $f(f(xy)+2xy)$, so
$$3f(xy)+3c\,xy=3x\,f(y)+3y\,f(x),$$
hence
$$\boxed{\,f(xy)+c\,xy=x\,f(y)+y\,f(x)\,}\qquad\forall x,y>0.\tag{2}$$

### Step 2. Reduction to Cauchy's logarithmic equation

Define $F(x)=f(x)-cx$. Then $F(1)=0$, and from (2):
$$F(xy)=f(xy)-cxy=xf(y)+yf(x)-2cxy=xF(y)+yF(x).\tag{3}$$

Set $G(x)=F(x)/x$ for $x>0$. Dividing (3) by $xy$:
$$G(xy)=G(x)+G(y),\qquad \forall x,y>0.\tag{4}$$

So $G$ satisfies the logarithmic Cauchy equation on $(0,\infty)$, and
$$f(x)=cx+x\,G(x)=x\bigl(c+G(x)\bigr).\tag{5}$$

### Step 3. Positivity forces $G\equiv 0$

Since $f(x)>0$ and $x>0$, (5) gives $c+G(x)>0$, i.e.
$$G(x)>-c\qquad\forall x>0.\tag{6}$$

Define $\phi:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ by $\phi(t)=G(e^t)$. From (4),
$$\phi(s+t)=G(e^{s+t})=G(e^s e^t)=G(e^s)+G(e^t)=\phi(s)+\phi(t),$$
so $\phi$ is additive. By (6), $\phi(t)>-c$ for every $t\in\mathbb R$. Using additivity, $\phi(-t)=-\phi(t)>-c$, hence $\phi(t)<c$ for every $t$. Thus
$$-c<\phi(t)<c\qquad\forall t\in\mathbb R:$$
$\phi$ is an additive function bounded on all of $\mathbb R$. A standard result on Cauchy's equation says an additive function bounded (above or below) on any nontrivial interval is linear, $\phi(t)=kt$; the only linear function bounded on $\mathbb R$ is $k=0$. Therefore $\phi\equiv 0$, and consequently
$$G\equiv 0,\qquad F\equiv 0,\qquad f(x)=cx.\tag{7}$$

### Step 4. Determine $c$

With $f(x)=cx$, substitute into (1) (equivalently into (P)):
$$f\bigl(f(u)+2u\bigr)=f(cu+2u)=f\bigl((c+2)u\bigr)=c(c+2)u,$$
while the right–hand side of (1) is $3cu+3cu=6cu$. Since $u>0$ and $c>0$,
$$c(c+2)=6c\;\Longrightarrow\;c+2=6\;\Longrightarrow\;c=4.$$

### Step 5. Verification

For $f(x)=4x$:
$$\text{LHS}=f\bigl(4xy+2xy\bigr)=f(6xy)=24xy,$$
$$\text{RHS}=3x\cdot 4y+3y\cdot 4x=12xy+12xy=24xy.$$
Both sides agree for all $x,y>0$, and $4x>0$ on $(0,\infty)$. ✓

---

**Conclusion.** The unique function satisfying the equation is
$$\boxed{f(x)=4x.}$$

